{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"class-templates","title":"Class Templates & Specialisation","group":"Templates & Generic Programming","order":43,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Class templates are how the STL is built: <code>std::vector&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>std::map&lt;K, V&gt;</code>, <code>std::array&lt;T, N&gt;</code>. The type parameter becomes part of the class's type.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>A generic container</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;stdexcept&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\ntemplate &lt;typename T&gt;\nclass Stack {\npublic:\n    void push(const T&amp; value) { items_.push_back(value); }\n    void pop() {\n        if (items_.empty()) throw std::out_of_range(\"pop on empty stack\");\n        items_.pop_back();\n    }\n    const T&amp; top() const { return items_.back(); }\n    bool empty() const { return items_.empty(); }\n    std::size_t size() const { return items_.size(); }\nprivate:\n    std::vector&lt;T&gt; items_;\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Stack&lt;int&gt; s;\n    s.push(1); s.push(2); s.push(3);\n    s.pop();\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; s.top() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; s.size() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    Stack&lt;std::string&gt; words;\n    words.push(\"hello\");\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; words.top() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>2 2\nhello</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-adjustments\"></i>Defaults, non-type parameters and CTAD</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;array&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;utility&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\ntemplate &lt;typename T, std::size_t Capacity = 4&gt;\nclass RingBuffer {\n    std::array&lt;T, Capacity&gt; data_{};\n    std::size_t head_ = 0, count_ = 0;\npublic:\n    void push(T v) {\n        data_[(head_ + count_) % Capacity] = v;\n        if (count_ &lt; Capacity) ++count_; else head_ = (head_ + 1) % Capacity;\n    }\n    T front() const { return data_[head_]; }\n    std::size_t size() const { return count_; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    RingBuffer&lt;int, 3&gt; rb;\n    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 5; ++i) rb.push(i);       // keeps the last 3\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; rb.front() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; rb.size() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    std::pair p{1, 2.5};                            // CTAD (C++17): pair&lt;int, double&gt;\n    std::vector v{1, 2, 3};                         // vector&lt;int&gt;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; p.second &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; v.size() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>3 3\n2.5 3</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-git-fork\"></i>Full and partial specialisation</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n\ntemplate &lt;typename T&gt;\nstruct TypeName { static const char* get() { return \"unknown\"; } };\n\ntemplate &lt;&gt;                                   // full specialisation\nstruct TypeName&lt;int&gt; { static const char* get() { return \"int\"; } };\n\ntemplate &lt;typename T&gt;                         // partial specialisation: any pointer\nstruct TypeName&lt;T*&gt; { static const char* get() { return \"pointer\"; } };\n\ntemplate &lt;typename K, typename V&gt;\nstruct Pair { const char* kind() const { return \"general\"; } };\ntemplate &lt;typename T&gt;\nstruct Pair&lt;T, T&gt; { const char* kind() const { return \"same types\"; } };   // partial\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; TypeName&lt;int&gt;::get() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; TypeName&lt;double*&gt;::get() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; TypeName&lt;char&gt;::get() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; Pair&lt;int, double&gt;{}.kind() &lt;&lt; \", \" &lt;&lt; Pair&lt;int, int&gt;{}.kind() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>int pointer unknown\ngeneral, same types</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Function templates</th><th>Class templates</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Full specialisation</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Partial specialisation</td><td><strong>No</strong> (overload instead)</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Deduce arguments</td><td>Yes (from call)</td><td>C++17 CTAD (from constructor)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code-dots\"></i>Member templates &amp; <code>typename</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;list&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\ntemplate &lt;typename Container&gt;\ntypename Container::value_type first(const Container&amp; c) {   // `typename`: a dependent *type*\n    return *c.begin();\n}\n\nstruct Printer {\n    template &lt;typename T&gt;\n    void print(const T&amp; x) const { std::cout &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; '\\n'; }   // member function template\n};\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; first(std::vector&lt;int&gt;{7, 8}) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; first(std::list&lt;char&gt;{'z'}) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    Printer{}.print(3.5);\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>7 z\n3.5</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why do you sometimes need the typename keyword inside a template?</summary><div><p>In <code>Container::value_type</code>, the compiler can't know whether <code>value_type</code> is a type or a static member until <code>Container</code> is known. By default it assumes a value; <code>typename</code> tells it the dependent name is a type.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between full and partial specialisation?</summary><div><p>Full specialisation fixes <em>all</em> template parameters (<code>TypeName&lt;int&gt;</code>); partial specialisation fixes some or constrains their form (<code>TypeName&lt;T*&gt;</code>, <code>Pair&lt;T, T&gt;</code>) and is still a template. Only class (and variable) templates can be partially specialised.</p></div></details></section>"}